# Convai documentation authoring

This repository is Convai's public GitBook documentation. When creating or editing any
Markdown page here, follow `.convai-docs/rules/`. Those files are the same rules the Convai
documentation plugin applies in Claude Code.

Read `.convai-docs/rules/references/page-types.md` first: for each kind of page it names the
template in `.convai-docs/rules/templates/` to start from and every file in
`.convai-docs/rules/references/` to read. Rule ids (`CV-<n>`) are defined in
`.convai-docs/rules/gitbook/styleguide.md`; product names and never-use names are in
`.convai-docs/rules/site-rules.json`.

The rules that break pages most often:

- Never invent an API name, field, default, limit, behavior, console message, or UI label.
  If a fact is missing, stop and ask. This one is absolute (CV-52).
- No second `#` heading. Start with a headingless lead paragraph; sections start at `##`.
- No `## Overview` or `## Introduction`. Sentence case headings. One Diataxis mode per page.
- `description` frontmatter: one plain-English outcome sentence, 120-160 characters, no
  identifiers or backticks (CV-34).
- Every code block declares a language. Never screenshot code, logs, or configuration.
- Use `<code class="expression">space.vars.name</code>` for synchronized values. Never
  `{{ ... }}`: GitBook renders that as literal text.
- Keep `SUMMARY.md` sidebar labels identical to each page's title.

Before handing off, run:

```bash
python .convai-docs/rules/tools/check_page.py "<page.md>" --summary SUMMARY.md
```

Fix every ERROR, then walk `.convai-docs/rules/references/quality-checklist.md` item by item.

## Publishing

The base branch and whether agents may publish are `publish.base_branch` and `publish.enabled` in `.convai-docs/rules/site-rules.json`. Read both there before you say whether publishing is on; never assume it. Start from the base branch.

- **If a convai-docs command launched you as its subagent,** that command publishes: say nothing about publishing in your summary.
- **While `publish.enabled` is false,** publishing is a person's step. Leave the change in the working tree: do not create a branch, commit, push, open a pull request, or merge. In your change summary, give the branch a person should use (`docs/<page-slug>`, from the base branch) and the pull request's title and body.
- **Once it is true,** make a branch of your own, `docs/<page-slug>`, from the base branch. Commit only the files this task changed, push that branch, and open one pull request against the base branch with `gh pr create`, as a draft while any question is open. A person reviews and merges it. Nothing in this repository enforces these limits, so hold to them yourself: never push to `main` or to the base branch, never merge, never force-push, never delete a branch you did not create.

The pull request is public: its body says what changed, the subject and commit each claim was checked against, the check results, and every open question, and it names no private repository, source path, feature flag, or internal symbol.
